%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level3_TheDigitalTrap
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_3
  levelNumber: 3
  displayName: The Digital Trap
  sceneName: Room3_DigitalTrap
  description: Convincing messages in a cybersecurity operations room. Compare each
    one with the evidence before you decide.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: de67521957d88cb478527ddaa3a59230, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: "LEVEL 3 \u2014 THE DIGITAL TRAP"
  briefingText: Welcome to the next stage of your investigation. These messages are
    more convincing, and some contain only subtle warning signs. Examine the sender,
    verify the context, and investigate suspicious requests before making your decision.
  caseNotes:
  - This morning you signed in on a new workstation, OPS-WS3.
  - You ordered a wireless headset (order ED-48213). Delivery is due tomorrow.
  - You requested a password change for your Example Cloud Service account.
  - The case-files computer by the west wall lists trusted contacts, orders, account
    activity and the IT policy.
  objectives:
  - Investigate six messages.
  - Classify each message as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
  - Examine suspicious details before answering.
  - Complete the investigation to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: l3_signin_alert
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Cloud Security
    senderDetail: security-alerts@examplecloud.example
    subject: Unusual Sign-In Activity
    body: We noticed a sign-in to your account from a new device. If this was you,
      no action is needed. If you do not recognize this activity, open the official
      application and review your recent sign-ins.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The notification comes from the fictional cloud service used
      by the player. Its sender information matches the trusted service details provided
      in the investigation environment.
    explanation: Security alerts can be legitimate. The message provides a safe way
      to investigate the activity through the official application and does not pressure
      you to disclose your password.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: Nothing if the sign-in was you; otherwise review recent sign-ins
      in the official app.
    linkNote: No link. It tells you to open the official application yourself.
    clues:
    - Reports a sign-in from a new device.
    - Does not ask for a password, code or payment.
    - Compare the sender's domain and the sign-in with the case files.
  - id: l3_lookalike_bank
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Bank Security
    senderDetail: security@examplebank-protect.example
    subject: 'Action Required: Account Protection'
    body: Our monitoring system detected unusual activity. To prevent temporary restrictions,
      confirm your account information through our verification page immediately.
    link: examplebank-protect.example/verify
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The sender's domain does not match the verified bank domain, and
      the message pressures you to act quickly. Do not use the supplied link. Contact
      the bank through its official application or a verified website.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Confirm your account information on a verification page, immediately.
    linkNote: examplebank-protect.example/verify (training placeholder, never opened).
    clues:
    - The display name says Example Bank Security.
    - Warns of restrictions unless you act immediately.
    - Compare the full sender domain with the Trusted Contacts file.
  - id: l3_it_password
    channel: 1
    sender: IT Support Desk
    senderDetail: it.support@yourcompany.example
    subject: Required Security Verification
    body: We are updating our security records. Please reply with your current password
      so we can confirm your account and avoid interruption to your access.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Legitimate support teams should not ask you to disclose your password
      by email. Verify the request through an established IT support channel and
      never send credentials in a message.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: Reply to this email with your current password.
    linkNote: No link. The reply goes back to the sender.
    clues:
    - The address shows the company domain - addresses can be spoofed.
    - Warns that your access may be interrupted.
    - Check what the IT Security Policy says about passwords.
  - id: l3_delivery_sms
    channel: 0
    sender: Example Delivery
    senderDetail: Saved contact
    subject: 
    body: Your delivery is scheduled for tomorrow. Your order reference is included
      in your official order history. Open the delivery app you normally use to view
      the latest status.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player previously placed an order in the simulated environment.
      The reference number matches the order shown in the trusted order history.
    explanation: The message matches a transaction you initiated, and its information
      is consistent with the order record. Checking through the official delivery
      app is safer than relying on an unfamiliar link.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: Check the delivery status in the delivery app you already use.
    linkNote: No link.
    clues:
    - Notification preview shows order reference ED-48213.
    - Sent from a saved contact.
    - Compare the reference with the Orders file.
  - id: l3_exec_giftcards
    channel: 1
    sender: Operations Director
    senderDetail: ops.director@yourcompany-exec.example
    subject: "Confidential \u2014 Urgent Request"
    body: I need you to purchase several digital gift vouchers for a client meeting.
      Please keep this confidential and send the redemption codes as soon as possible.
      I am currently unavailable for a call.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The request combines urgency, secrecy, an unusual payment method,
      and avoidance of normal verification. Confirm unusual financial requests through
      a separate, trusted communication channel.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Buy digital gift vouchers and email the redemption codes.
    linkNote: No link.
    clues:
    - Signed with a job title only.
    - Asks you to keep it confidential and says a call isn't possible.
    - Compare the domain with your company's domain.
  - id: l3_password_confirm
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Cloud Service
    senderDetail: no-reply@examplecloud.example
    subject: Password Change Confirmation
    body: The password change you requested has been completed. If you made this
      change, no further action is needed. If you did not request it, open the official
      application and review your account security.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player requested a password change in the simulated investigation
      setup.
    explanation: The notification matches an action you initiated and provides a
      safe method to review your account. If the change were unexpected, you should
      investigate through the official application.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: Nothing if you made the change; otherwise review security in
      the official app.
    linkNote: No link.
    clues:
    - Confirms a password change.
    - Suggests opening the official application yourself.
    - Compare with the Account Activity file.
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l3_old_welcome
    channel: 1
    sender: Security Team
    senderDetail: security@yourcompany.example
    subject: Welcome to the operations room
    body: Your workstation OPS-WS3 is ready. The case-files computer by the west
      wall holds trusted contacts, order history, account activity and the IT policy.
      Compare every flagged message with it before you decide.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
  - id: l3_old_statement
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Bank
    senderDetail: statements@examplebank.example
    subject: Your monthly statement is ready
    body: Your statement is available in the Example Bank app. We will never ask
      for your password or full card number by email.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
  - id: l3_old_order
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Delivery
    senderDetail: orders@exampledelivery.example
    subject: Order ED-48213 confirmed
    body: 'Thanks for your order: wireless headset. We''ll text you from our saved
      number when it''s on its way.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
  caseFiles:
  - title: Trusted Contacts
    body: "Verified sender domains (from your onboarding pack):\n\u2022  Example
      Bank \u2014 <b>examplebank.example</b>\n\u2022  Example Cloud Service / Example
      Cloud Security \u2014 <b>examplecloud.example</b>\n\u2022  Example Delivery
      \u2014 <b>exampledelivery.example</b>, texts from the saved contact \"Example
      Delivery\"\n\u2022  Your company \u2014 <b>yourcompany.example</b> (all staff,
      including IT and directors)\n\nCompare the whole domain after the @, letter
      by letter. A display name can say anything."
  - title: Orders
    body: "Your recent orders and transactions:\n\u2022  <b>ED-48213</b> \u2014 Wireless
      headset \u2014 Example Delivery \u2014 out for delivery, arriving tomorrow\n\u2022 
      ED-47790 \u2014 Phone case \u2014 Example Delivery \u2014 delivered last week\n\u2022 
      Example Bank \u2014 no pending payments, no open support requests\n\u2022 
      Company purchasing \u2014 no voucher or gift card purchases on record"
  - title: Account Activity
    body: "Example Cloud Service \u2014 today:\n\u2022  08:15  Sign-in from a new
      device: <b>OPS-WS3</b> (this operations workstation)\n\u2022  08:42  Password
      change requested by you\n\u2022  08:43  Password change completed\n\nExample
      Bank \u2014 today:\n\u2022  No sign-ins or account changes"
  - title: IT Policy
    body: "IT Security Policy (fictional):\n\u2022  IT Support will never ask for
      your password \u2014 not by email, phone or chat.\n\u2022  Password resets
      happen only in the self-service portal you already use.\n\u2022  Any purchase
      or payment needs approval in the finance system, never by email alone.\n\u2022 
      Gift cards and vouchers are not a payment method for company business.\n\u2022 
      Report suspicious messages to the security team with the Report button."
  - title: Notes
    body: "Investigator notes \u2014 Case 03:\n\u2022  The Operations Director is
      travelling today but can be reached on the company chat and phone.\n\u2022 
      Earlier lookalike domains added a word or a hyphen next to a real brand name.\n\u2022 
      A real alert tells you to use the official app; a fake one sends you to its
      own page.\n\u2022  Ask yourself: did I start this? Does it match my records?"
  firstMessageDelay: 10
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 10, y: 20}
  minimumActivityDelay: 5
  oneStarScore: 1
  twoStarScore: 300
  threeStarScore: 480
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 300
  nextLevelId: level_4
  nextSceneName: Room4_DigitalForensicsLab
  lessons:
  - 'Security alerts can be real: judge them by what they ask and where they send
    you.'
  - Compare the full sender address with trusted records - display names are easy
    to fake.
  - No genuine support team needs your password, even from a familiar-looking address.
  - 'Urgency, secrecy and gift cards together mean: verify through a separate channel.'
  - Messages that match actions you started are more likely legitimate - still check
    in the official app.
  learningSummary: You compared messages against trusted records, spotted a lookalike
    domain, a credential request and an executive impersonation, and recognised genuine
    alerts that matched your own activity.
  performanceHigh: Outstanding analysis! You weighed every clue against the evidence
    and made sharp decisions.
  performanceModerate: Solid investigation. Review the explanations - the subtle
    cases reward checking the case files.
  performanceLow: Keep practising. Compare each sender and request with the case
    files before you decide.
